<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Protest and freedom · 1879 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Warszawianka (Whirlwinds of Danger)</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="pl">Warszawianka</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Polish workers' song from about 1880, written by a man in prison, later sung in Russian, English and Spanish.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Wacław Święcicki</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Józef Pławiński</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Polish socialists and the workers' movement</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Warsaw, Poland (Russian partition)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>between 1879 and 1883</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Wacław Święcicki, 1879 or 1883; widely sung by the 1890s)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warszawianka">Warszawianka</a>&quot; is a Polish socialist revolutionary song written between 1879 and 1883. Its title, which means a woman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> (and can be rendered &quot;The Varsovian&quot; or &quot;The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Warsaw&quot;), deliberately recalls an earlier Polish song of the same name; to tell them apart it is often called &quot;Warszawianka of 1905&quot;, after it became the anthem of the workers' protests of the Revolution in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> (1905–1907), when 30 workers were shot at the May Day demonstrations in Warsaw in 1905.</p><p>By one account Wacław Święcicki wrote the words in 1879 while imprisoned for socialist activity in the Tenth Pavilion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> Citadel; by another he wrote them in 1883, just after returning from exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. The music is by Józef Pławiński, who was imprisoned with him, and draws partly on &quot;Marsz Żuawów&quot;, a song of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/january-uprising">January Uprising</a>. By the early 1890s it was one of the most popular revolutionary songs in Russian-ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><p>The song spread across languages and causes. A Russian version, &quot;Varshavianka&quot;, which removed every mention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, is usually credited to Gleb Krzhizhanovsky and dated to 1897, when he was in prison; it became very popular. In the 1920s Douglas Robson of the Industrial Workers of the World wrote English words, &quot;March <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the Workers&quot;, better known as &quot;Whirlwinds of Danger&quot;; &quot;Rufus John&quot; Goss recorded it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> around 1925, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-robeson">Paul Robeson</a> sang its first verse and Leon Lishner the whole, and in 1938 a version with new second and third verses by Randall Swingler was published. In the early 1930s Valeriano Orobón Fernández made the Spanish &quot;A las barricadas&quot;, one of the most popular songs of the Spanish anarchists in the Spanish Civil War, and in the early 2010s the Burmese student activist Zin Linn wrote a Burmese version from the Spanish. The army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> marched to a German translation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s 1st <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parachute">Parachute</a> Hussar Regiment adopted the tune with other words. Isadora Duncan made a dance, &quot;Varshavianka&quot;, to it in 1924.</p><p>The tune is heard in films: Battleship Potemkin (1925), in scenes of Odesa in the Revolution of 1905; The Youth of Maxim, sung by demonstrating workers; Hostile Whirlwinds (1953), named after the first words of the Russian version; Doctor Zhivago; The Jackal; Hail, Caesar!; and the Polish series 1983 (2018). The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> Revolutionary Choir sings it in Polish and Spanish in turn.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A socialist revolutionary march of the workers' movement; defiant and solemn.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Polish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Polish</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Śmiało podnieśmy sztandar nasz w górę!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Boldly let us raise our banner high!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Choć burza wrogich żywiołów wyje,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Though the storm of hostile elements howls,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Choć nas dziś gnębią siły ponure,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Though grim forces oppress us today,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Chociaż niepewne jutro niczyje...</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Though tomorrow is certain for no one...</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">O!... bo to sztandar całej ludzkości,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Oh!... for it is the banner of all mankind,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">To hasło święte, pieśń zmartwychwstania,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is the holy watchword, the song of resurrection,</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">To tryumf pracy — sprawiedliwości,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is the triumph of labour — of justice,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">To zorza wszystkich ludów zbratania.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is the dawn of the brotherhood of all peoples.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Naprzód, Warszawo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Forward, Warsaw!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Na walkę krwawą,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To the bloody fight,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Świętą a prawą!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Holy and just!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Marsz, marsz, Warszawo!...</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>March, march, Warsaw!...</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Dziś, gdy roboczy lud ginie z głodu,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Today, when the working people are dying of hunger,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Zbrodnią w rozkoszy tonąć, jak w błocie;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To drown in luxury is a crime, as in mud;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">I hańba temu, kto z nas za młodu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And shame on him among us who in his youth</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Lęka się stanąć choć na szafocie!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Is afraid to stand even on the scaffold!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Nikt za ideę nie ginie marnie,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>No one dies in vain for an idea,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Z czasem zwycięża Chrystus Judasza!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In time Christ overcomes Judas!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Niech święty ogień młodość ogarnie,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let holy fire seize the young,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Choć wielu padnie — lecz przyszłość nasza!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Though many will fall — yet the future is ours!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Naprzód, Warszawo! itd. itd.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Forward, Warsaw! etc. etc.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Hura!... zerwijmy z carów korony,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hurrah!... let us tear the crowns from the tsars,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Gdy ludy dotąd chodzą w cierniowej;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>While the peoples still go in crowns of thorns;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">I w krwi zatopmy nadgniłe trony,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And let us drown the half-rotten thrones in blood,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Spurpurowione we krwi ludowej!...</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Crimsoned in the blood of the people!...</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Ha!... zemsta straszna dzisiejszym katom,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ha!... terrible vengeance on today's executioners,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Co wysysają życie z miljonów!...</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who suck the life out of millions!...</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Ha!... zemsta carom i plutokratom!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ha!... vengeance on the tsars and plutocrats!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">A przyjdzie żniwo przyszłości plonów!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And the harvest of the future's crops will come!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Naprzód, Warszawo! itd.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Forward, Warsaw! etc.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Wacław Święcicki (1879 or 1883). Text from <a href="https://pl.wikisource.org/wiki/Warszawianka_(%C5%9Amia%C5%82o_podnie%C5%9Bmy_sztandar_nasz_w_g%C3%B3r%C4%99...)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>line 4.2: 'Zbrodnią w rozkoszy tonąć, jak w błocie' is elliptical: 'it is a crime to drown in pleasure (luxury) as in mud'.</li><li>line 4.9 and 5.9: 'itd.' (i tak dalej, 'and so on') marks the refrain of stanza 3 to be sung again; rendered 'etc.'.</li><li>line 5.2: 'chodzą w cierniowej' leaves the noun unsaid: 'go in a thorny [crown]', i.e. a crown of thorns, set against the tsars' crowns.</li><li>line 5.8: 'żniwo przyszłości plonów' = literally 'the harvest of the crops of the future'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="jvz5IGkWsE8" data-title="Warszawianka (Whirlwinds of Danger): sung in Russian by the Alexandrov Ensemble"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">sung in Russian by the Alexandrov Ensemble</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel The Red Army Choir - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jvz5IGkWsE8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Varshavianka (Gleb Krzhizhanovsky)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1897</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Russian version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Varshavianka (Isadora Duncan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1924</span><span class="sg-chain-h">dance to the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Whirlwinds of Danger / March Song of the Workers (Douglas Robson)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1920s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Whirlwinds of Danger (Randall Swingler's verses)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1938</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English version</span></li><li><a href="/songs/a-las-barricadas">A las barricadas (Valeriano Orobón Fernández)</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">early 1930s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Spanish version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Burmese version (Zin Linn)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">early 2010s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation from the Spanish</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">German version (East German army)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">1st Parachute Hussar Regiment march</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, other words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Battleship Potemkin (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1925</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in (instrumental)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hostile Whirlwinds (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1953</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title taken from the Russian version's first words</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Warszawianka (1831)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1831</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title deliberately recalls it</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Marsz Żuawów (song of the January Uprising)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody drawn on in part</span></li></ul></section>
